# Scientific Integrity

Scientific claims must be evidence-driven.

Never equate trajectory fit with mechanism recovery.

Always distinguish:

- predictive fit
- mechanistic recovery
- parameter identifiability
- functional identifiability
- uncertainty
- extrapolation
- symbolic support
- biological/physical validity

Never call a practical Fisher/Jacobian diagnostic a structural
identifiability certificate.

Never claim functional identifiability unless a functional-identifiability
procedure has actually been implemented and validated.

Never weaken thresholds, alter benchmark definitions, remove difficult
seeds, or modify evaluation data merely to make CI pass.

Never hide failed optimization, failed solver runs, or failed recovery cases.

Do not invent scientific novelty.

Before claiming a methodological contribution is new, investigate
closely related literature.

A negative scientific result is valid.

If the available evidence cannot distinguish mechanisms, report ambiguity
rather than forcing a single answer.

Do not call a heuristic confidence number a statistical confidence interval
unless its statistical meaning is justified.

Do not claim biological causality merely from graph locality.

Do not claim biological constants when parameter/function scale is
non-identifiable.

Every major scientific claim should have corresponding tests and,
where appropriate, reproducible benchmark evidence.